WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.340
This is an exam paper from the University&nbsp;
of Cambridge it's part of a course which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:11.820
is widely regarded as being the most demanding&nbsp;
undergraduate mathematics course in Britain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:18.000
that is the Mathematical Tripos. The tripos&nbsp;
comes in three parts spread over three years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.740
and it is an old exam steeped in tradition&nbsp;
and prestige. It was introduced around 1790&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.380
and still challenges some of the smartest&nbsp;
students today. Some mathematicians would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.860
like to see it abolished but for now the tripos&nbsp;
still has a strong grip on English higher math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.800
education so let's take a look at it. This is&nbsp;
the exam that for over 200 years has been seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.900
as a leading test of mental agility and&nbsp;
stamina. It's changed a lot in that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.840
but the current iteration still contains all&nbsp;
sorts of really difficult questions. It starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.880
with number theory; compute the continued&nbsp;
fraction expansion of the square root of 29.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.320
Then an analysis proof then a cryptography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.460
question asking one to describe the&nbsp;
Berlekamp-Massey method for breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.120
a cipher stream arising from a&nbsp;
linear feedback shift register.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.580
This is one of the four papers which a student&nbsp;
would take in their third year of the tripos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:24.780
After this they can leave with a bachelor's degree&nbsp;
or do one more year of study called part three to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.840
obtain a masters of math. You'll notice though&nbsp;
that this one exam paper contains questions on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:37.320
everything from number theory to coding to&nbsp;
statistics, quantum mechanics and analysis.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.180
It’s more common elsewhere to see these&nbsp;
different areas of math separated into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.140
different modular exams where you&nbsp;
only need to study for one at a time.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.780
That’s not the case here, all the subjects are&nbsp;
considered together but you do get to pick and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.480
chose which questions to tackle. This approach&nbsp;
allows students to attend whatever courses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.680
and lectures they wish and then choose&nbsp;
how they want to prepare for the exam.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.600
Heres the list of courses on offer in&nbsp;
third year. I’m drawn to see what exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.040
questions look like for some of these,&nbsp;
for example math of machine learning.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:17.040
This is the 2022 question they are asked&nbsp;
to write down and adaboost algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.680
to say what is meant by a convex hull&nbsp;
and a convex combination of vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:29.520
what is meant by the empirical Rademacher&nbsp;
complexity. Here's the syllabus for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.800
mathematics of machine learning course to see&nbsp;
the other kind of ideas that go into this math.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.800
Cambridge now has many triposes for several&nbsp;
different courses including an english tripos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:47.940
and natural sciences tripos but it used to be&nbsp;
just math. The exam originally took the form of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:55.260
debate where the student had to wrangle logic but&nbsp;
then became what might be the oldest university&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:02.700
written exam in the world. The original exam&nbsp;
was called the Senate House exam because that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:07.500
the name of the building where it was taken. 
 
The oldest copy of the exam that I could find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.560
was this one from 1841. The questions&nbsp;
are also difficult, but different

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:20.160
Solve the following equations and show that a&nbsp;
quadratic equation cannot have more than two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.640
roots. Prove that in a spherical triangle any&nbsp;
one side is greater than the difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:33.180
the two others. Enunciate and prove the tenth&nbsp;
Lemma of Newton's first section. They expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:39.180
students to be familiar with Newton's work in&nbsp;
a way that no student is today if I pull out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:44.820
my handy Principia we might be able to get the&nbsp;
answer to this one. Okay this looks like Lemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:50.700
10 in the first section; The spaces which a&nbsp;
body describes by a finite force urging it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:58.440
whether that force is determined and immutable or&nbsp;
is continually augmented or continually diminished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:07.200
are in the very beginning of the motion to each&nbsp;
other as the squares of the times. hmm. The focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.200
on Newton does make sense since Newton did study&nbsp;
in Cambridge and heavily influenced the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.380
math scene. Give the arguments for supposing&nbsp;
the earth to be round and on the supposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:26.340
of its being a sphere investigate a formula for&nbsp;
calculating approximately the dip of the horizon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:34.140
This older exam was a true mental agility and&nbsp;
endurance test there are 12 papers to be taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:40.680
two a day for six days it's a week-long marathon&nbsp;
answering hundreds of questions for five and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:46.500
half hours a day students trained for it like&nbsp;
an Olympic sport. The highest scoring student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:53.400
was called the Senior Wrangler and became a minor&nbsp;
celebrity. I found the scores for an exam in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.400 --> 00:05:01.020
1860s which gives an idea of scoring around&nbsp;
this time and out of a possible mark of 17000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:11.160
the senior Wrangler scored only about 7600,&nbsp;
second place scored about 4100 and the lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:20.040
scoring candidate to still pass scored just 237&nbsp;
points and earned the special Wooden Spoon award.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:27.000
The 1841 results are listed here with Stokes&nbsp;
the Senior Wrangler. That is George Stokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.360
now famous for the Navier-Stokes equation in&nbsp;
fluid mechanics and Stokes' theorem in vector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.420
calculus. You might have been examined on those&nbsp;
ideas yourself and now you've seen the exact exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:46.380
questions Stokes faced as a student. Let's compare&nbsp;
some similar questions from then and now. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.080
that this exam took place before Einstein's work&nbsp;
on relativity we can't expect much of an analog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:59.100
to this general relativity question but there are&nbsp;
plenty of questions about Newtonian gravity. 1841&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:06.060
was before we developed astronomical spectroscopy&nbsp;
or measured the expansion of the universe so this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:13.440
cosmology question would blow the mind of even&nbsp;
an 1841 Senior Wrangler- but wait actually George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.900
Stokes apparently is credited with inventing&nbsp;
the fundamental principles of spectroscopy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:25.800
Modern quantum mechanics wasn't to be invented&nbsp;
for another 60 years so we won't see that but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.960
there are plenty of questions about light&nbsp;
and we won't find much machine learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:37.620
statistical modeling or coding and cryptography&nbsp;
in the 1841 paper since there were no computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.740
These are the best I could find to resemble the&nbsp;
modern number theory and analysis questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:49.800
Training for the tripos has historically&nbsp;
involved external coaching to help students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:55.320
increase the speed that they can solve and&nbsp;
write out solutions and the whole process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:01.560
has been criticized for not allowing enough&nbsp;
time for a deep understanding or to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:07.200
investigations which are important skills&nbsp;
for mathematician and the Senior Wrangler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.300
has not always turned out to be the most&nbsp;
distinguished mathematician later in life.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:19.380
One notable critic of the tripos was G H Hardy,&nbsp;
Ramanujan's mentor, Hardy gave an address to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:26.460
mathematical Association in 1926 calling to&nbsp;
abolish the tripos completely he said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:32.700
the ability to show originality in mathematics&nbsp;
can't be tested under exam conditions and so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:38.460
exam reduces to a kind of game. A student can&nbsp;
choose to play the game with all their force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.080
but must realize they are putting their&nbsp;
mathematical development on hold to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:49.380
so. The prestige and status of the tripos forces&nbsp;
many students to devote everything to the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.860
Hardy isn't against exams in general and knows&nbsp;
they are useful to test students but he wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:01.380
exams that do not profess to be more than a&nbsp;
reasonable test of certain humdrum qualities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:07.800
The qualities worth testing on an exam would&nbsp;
carry any student at Cambridge to second class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:15.360
and beyond that he does not believe in recognizing&nbsp;
differences of ability by examination. The tripos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:22.140
is an exam that attempted to sort students right&nbsp;
to the top, today students are no longer publicly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:26.760
ranked in order but the Senior Wrangler&nbsp;
is still acknowledged during the reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:33.660
of results at Senate House. Students today might&nbsp;
still relate to the idea of having to put their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:40.320
real mathematical pursuits on hold in order&nbsp;
to play the game. Hardy says if Einstein had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:46.200
taken the tripos what would it matter what place&nbsp;
he took? Students are driven to perform in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:53.520
exams by tutors and greedy college authorities. "I&nbsp;
do not want to reform the tripos but destroy it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:59.880
but neither I nor anyone else will succeed in&nbsp;
doing so since the practical difficulties would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:06.180
be so serious and the force of tradition is&nbsp;
so terribly strong." Some things have changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:14.280
since 1926. Hardy's view is that the tripos should&nbsp;
produce research mathematicians but not everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:21.480
studying math has that aim. The modern day tripos&nbsp;
website is proud that the course prepares students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:27.300
for a range of jobs not just academia. Let&nbsp;
me show you one more interesting past paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:35.820
this was the first page of the tripos exam in 1890&nbsp;
and was the exam taken by Philippa Fawcett. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:42.000
wasn't the first woman to sit the math tripos but&nbsp;
was the first to rank number one. As the results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:48.060
of the exam were announced the men's results were&nbsp;
read first and then the women's. Women weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:54.180
officially ranked but were placed relative to the&nbsp;
men. Fawcett was placed above the Senior Wrangler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.180 --> 00:10:01.920
and Senate House erupted in celebration. She beat&nbsp;
the Senior Wrangler by 400 marks and her success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:08.160
was headline news. It took another 58 years&nbsp;
before women were actually allowed to receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:14.160
degrees from Cambridge. Today the results are read&nbsp;
out in alphabetical order rather than by rank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:21.180
but what do you think does the tripos deserve to&nbsp;
be destroyed as Hardy wanted or is it a worthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:27.600
challenge that motivates students to perform at&nbsp;
their best? Thanks for watching and a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:34.020
thank you to my Patreon supporters including&nbsp;
today's Patron cats of the day Pan and Tilt.

